The old “buyer beware” mindset doesn’t carry much weight anymore.

Today, manufacturers are expected to stand behind their products, especially when it comes to big purchases like cars. When they make promises through a written warranty, they’re legally responsible for making things right if the product doesn’t work as it should.

In California, these protections go a step further. The [Song-Beverly Consumer Warranty Act](https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?division=3.&amp&chapter=1.&amp&part=4.&amp&lawCode=CIV&amp&title=1.7.&amp&article=3.) is one of the strongest consumer laws in the country. If your warranty-covered vehicle keeps breaking down - and the issue isn’t resolved after a reasonable number of repair attempts - you could be dealing with a warranty breach under the Song-Beverly Consumer Warranty Act.

But what exactly does a warranty breach involve? And how can you tell if your issues meet the legal standard?

Let’s go over it, step-by-step, so you know where you stand and what your next steps should be.

## What Is a Warranty Breach?

A warranty breach occurs when a consumer good (in our case, a car) falls short of the promises laid out in its warranty.

Under the Song-Beverly Consumer Warranty Act, a breach usually means one or more of the following:

- The car doesn’t function the way the warranty says it should

- The dealer or manufacturer can’t fix the issue after multiple attempts

- The defect affects how you use the product, how safe it is, or what it’s worth

- Repairs take too long or keep getting delayed without resolution

Your car doesn’t have to break down completely. It’s enough if the problem seriously interferes with your ability to drive it, trust it, or maintain its resale value. Some common examples include [transmission problems](/blog/faulty-car-transmission-inspection), brake failures, electrical issues, fuel system defects, or constant check engine lights that never get resolved.

## Express Vs. Implied Warranty Breaches

One of the best things about the Song-Beverly Consumer Warranty Act is that it covers more than just the fine print in your owner’s manual. It protects you under both express and implied warranties.

An express warranty is a written one. It includes factory warranties, powertrain coverage, and any extended protection plans the dealership or manufacturer provides. If the manufacturer promises to fix defects for a certain period and fails to do so, it can count as a warranty breach.

An implied warranty, on the other hand, is an automatic protection. Essentially, it says that any product sold in California should work as expected for a reasonable time.

For example, if a brand-new car stalls without warning - on the highway - within a few weeks of purchase, that could violate the [implied warranty of merchantability](https://codes.findlaw.com/ca/civil-code/civ-sect-1791-1/#:~:text=As%20used%20in%20this%20chapter,which%20such%20goods%20are%20used.), even if the issue isn’t specifically listed in the applicable express warranties.

## What Happens After a Warranty Breach?

Once it’s clear that the manufacturer has breached their warranty, you can take legal action by filing a lemon law claim while your car is still in the warranty period.

Here are the most common outcomes drivers see in these cases.

## Potential Buyback

The first option is for the manufacturer to refund your car’s purchase price, monthly payments, taxes, and other overheads. They may deduct a usage fee based on the miles you drove before the defect surfaced, but you’d otherwise get the bulk of your investment back.

## Possible Replacement

You may also be offered a replacement vehicle with similar features and value. If you’re happy with the make and model, this could be a fast way to move on from the issue. If not, you can turn it down and push for another resolution.

## Cash-and-Keep Settlement

Some manufacturers [offer a cash settlement](/blog/cash-and-keep-settlement) while letting you keep the vehicle. It’s a way to make up for the defect without returning the car - but keep in mind, the issue might still exist.

Before agreeing to anything, weigh the pros and cons. And don’t sign off on a deal until you’ve spoken to a California lemon law lawyer. You want to be sure you’re getting what you’re owed.

## What Does “Reasonable Number of Repair Attempts” Mean?

While CA lemon law doesn’t give a strict number for what qualifies as a “reasonable” number of repair attempts, it does lay out some common-sense benchmarks.

- Two or more repair attempts for a defect that could put your safety at risk.

- Four or more repair attempts for less serious issues that still affect how you use the car or impact its value.

- 30 or more cumulative days in the shop for any covered issue (or combination of issues) while the vehicle is still under warranty.

The idea behind a “reasonable number of attempts” is to give the seller or manufacturer a fair shot at fixing the problem. You can’t immediately demand a refund the first time something goes wrong. The law recognizes that defects sometimes need more than one repair or a bit of troubleshooting.

However, once the problem keeps coming back, or the dealer can’t find a permanent solution, things may tip in your favor. Contact a California lemon law lawyer to find out if your situation qualifies as a warranty breach under the Song-Beverly Consumer Warranty Act.

## Does it Matter if the Defect is Minor?

Not every rattle, squeak, or cosmetic flaw counts as a warranty breach. If it doesn’t affect safety, how you drive, or what the car is worth, it probably won’t qualify.

For instance, a loose door handle or a squeaky window can be inconvenient, but not enough to trigger a full buyback. However, a backup camera that flickers or fails could be taken more seriously if it blocks your view while reversing.

“Minor” issues can become serious when they compromise how you use the car or how safe it is. If you’re not sure whether your car’s defect crosses that line, talk to a California lemon law lawyer.

## What Can You Do If You Suspect a Warranty Breach?

Most manufacturers don’t build bad vehicles on purpose, but when they fail to fix a known defect or drag their feet instead of delivering a real solution, you don’t have to accept it. If they’ve broken the promise made in the car warranty, you have every right to hold them accountable and move forward with a claim.

Here’s what you should do.

## 1. Gather Every Repair Record

Hang onto all work orders, invoices, and notes from authorized service and repair facilities. These documents show what was fixed (or not fixed), how many times the problem happened, and how long your car was out of service.

## 2. Keep a Log of Conversations

Dealers and manufacturers don’t always put things in writing. If you’ve had any calls, in-person visits, or casual texts about the issue, jot them down. Include names, dates, and what was discussed.

## 3. Document How the Problem Affects You

Don’t rely on memory. Write down exactly how the defect is interfering with daily use, whether it’s missed work, safety concerns, rental costs, or anything else that adds stress or expense. Be specific. Courts take real-world impact seriously.

## 4. Speak with an Attorney

Yes, you can [file a warranty claim on your own](/blog/diy-lemon-law-vs-hiring-an-attorney). However, large automakers have entire legal teams devoted to lowering what they pay in lemon law disputes. They may offer unfairly low settlements or drag things out until you’re too frustrated to keep going.

To level the playing field, you need someone who knows how to push back strategically and effectively. A California lemon law lawyer can handle the paperwork, phone calls, negotiating, and the pressure of fighting back against a big-name brand.

Best of all, most attorneys work on a contingency basis. If you win, their fees are paid by the manufacturer. You get professional legal support without paying out of pocket.

